<p>
  The project started with one lizard. During a study of natural selection on
  brown anoles on islands in the Bahamas, every lizard had been caught,
  measured, marked and released. A year later Jonathan Losos was chasing one
  particularly nimble individual through the branches. Only once it was in hand did
  he realize that it was missing its entire left hind leg. A year earlier it had
  had all four. In between, the leg had been lost, the wound had healed, and the
  lizard was, as our paper puts it, &ldquo;fat and sassy.&rdquo;
</p>
<p>
  Over the years, more of these animals turned up, and conversations with
  colleagues revealed that field biologists working on many different lizards had
  seen the same thing. So we pooled the records.
</p>

<h2>What we found</h2>
<p>
  Together with field biologists around the world, we documented limb loss, from a
  single foot to an entire leg, in 58 lizard species. Every case had healed, and
  many of the lizards looked robust and healthy. Limb-deficient lizards are rare,
  typically less than 1% of a population. Yet where we could measure them, they
  often had body condition, sprint speed and survival comparable to their
  four-legged neighbors.
</p>

<h2>Why it matters for evolution</h2>
<p>
  Limbs are one of the classic examples of natural selection fitting animals to
  their environment. Across lizard species, limb length tracks habitat closely, and even
  small differences between individuals can affect how fast they run and whether
  they survive. By that logic a lizard missing a whole leg should not last long.
</p>
<blockquote>
  It may be said that natural selection is daily and hourly scrutinizing,
  throughout the world, every variation, even the slightest.
  <br>Charles Darwin, 1859
</blockquote>
<p>
  Three-legged lizards suggest that selection is not always that relentless. Traits
  that look essential may, at least some of the time, matter less for survival than
  we assume, which in turn shapes how we should design and interpret studies of
  adaptation in the wild. Is natural selection always as omnipresent as Darwin
  envisioned it to be? Our long-term work on <a href="/research/lizard-island/">Lizard
  Island</a>, where selection comes and goes from season to season, points in the
  same direction.
</p>
<p>
  The paper, &ldquo;Pirates of the Caribbean (and elsewhere),&rdquo; was published in
  <em>The American Naturalist</em> in 2025 and made the front page of the
  <em>New York Times</em> print edition. James Stroud and Jonathan Losos also wrote
  about it for <em>The Conversation</em>.
</p>
